  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h / 12-Day Gap since Day 81)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Commercial shipping in the Strait of Hormuz reached its lowest point since the conflict began. IMF PortWatch recorded 4 vessels transiting on May 24, approximately 4% of the pre-war 95–120 per day baseline. By May 30, IRGC reported roughly 24 ships in the prior 24 hours — a figure excluded from resolution counting under the PGSA ambiguity rule (dark-fleet, no Western insurer coverage). Lloyd's war-risk premiums remained at 5–8 times pre-war levels through May 6, and no major Western shipping line has announced any return to scheduled Hormuz routing. ADNOC CEO Sultan Al Jaber provided the most authoritative forward assessment available: full commercial flow would not return before Q1-Q2 2027, with 80% restoration requiring at minimum four months even if the conflict ended immediately. Against this baseline, the June 10 threshold of at least 50 transits per day is not achievable on any realistic timeline absent both a signed MOU and instantaneous, uncontested physical reopening, making the mine-removal clause a decisive logistics constraint regardless of diplomatic outcome. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The Iran-US negotiation track advanced further than at any prior point in the conflict, but has not crossed the principal-confirmation threshold required for resolution. Trump described the deal as "largely negotiated" on May 23 (CNN), then walked back expectations on May 24. On May 28, Axios and Al Jazeera reported the two sides had reached a tentative 60-day truce MOU — encompassing Hormuz declared unrestricted, Iranian mine removal in 30 days, proportional US naval blockade lift, and a nuclear commitments framework — contingent on Trump's final approval. Trump held a Situation Room meeting on May 29 without reaching a final determination. On May 31, Axios reports Trump sent the text back with edits targeting nuclear enrichment specifics, with US officials projecting approximately three days for Iranian response. As of May 31 EOD: the MOU text is approximately 80% agreed but no document has been signed or authenticated by principals. The strict ambiguity rule — requiring Trump and Pezeshkian (or his office), not envoys alone, to confirm publicly within 72 hours — combined with the edit-response-loop timeline makes EOD June 5 an extremely compressed window. N1 (p=0.35, June 7) captures the more probable near-term signing window. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Israeli domestic politics crossed a decisive threshold. Coalition whip Ofir Katz submitted the government's own dissolution bill approximately May 19–20; preliminary Knesset reading passed 110-0 on May 20 with co-sponsorship from UTJ (Degel HaTorah and Agudat Yisrael), Shas, the Religious Zionist Party, and Otzma Yehudit — every significant coalition faction. The coalition seat count is reported at approximately 60, one below the 61-seat governing threshold. A Channel 12 poll from May 29 projects the coalition at only 51 seats at a hypothetical election. First formal reading of the dissolution bill is scheduled for June 1. Netanyahu has not formally announced a specific election date; reporting positions him as targeting October. Assaf Shapira of the Israel Democracy Institute assessed September-October as the most likely election range, with August not feasible given the 90-day constitutional requirement. The F2 ambiguity rule has been updated with a voluntary-dissolution addendum: a government-initiated bill passing all formal readings by majority vote counts as YES regardless of which party initiated the process. N2 (p=0.65, second reading by June 15) tracks the procedural next step. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The Lebanon 45-day ceasefire extension (announced May 15) survived through and beyond the May 22 resolution horizon in formal terms. No US State Department withdrawal of ceasefire sponsorship was issued. No Lebanese government statement declared the truce void. IDF air operations continued at high intensity — Israeli aircraft conducted 2,847 recorded strikes on Lebanese territory between April 16 and May 26, killing at least 3,213 people per Lebanese health authorities, including six paramedics in strikes on Hanaway and Deir Qanoun an-Naher on May 22 — but these occurred outside Beirut governorate and below the sustained 50-launches-per-day threshold, placing them within the defined porous-truce pattern excluded from resolution. The IDF chief used language characterizing the ceasefire as non-existent in operational terms, and by May 26 declared all areas south of the Zahrani River active combat zones. The institutional analysis of Lebanon's capacity under these conditions is tracked separately by Middle East Monitor (May 28). Formal resolution: NO. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>F5 (Netanyahu formal election announcement by June 1) resolves NO at its horizon. The dissolution bill passed only the preliminary reading within the horizon window; three further formal readings are required. No Netanyahu formal Knesset speech naming a specific election date was recorded before EOD June 1. The dissolution process continues on a trajectory tracked by N2 (second reading by June 15, p=0.65) and F2 (coalition collapse before October 27, p=0.75). → supports <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
