      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (28%):</strong> Diplomatic track collapses at the principal-confirmation step after the Trump edit-loop, Hormuz closure extends through summer, and conflict returns to pre-negotiation intensity.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (48%):</strong> MOU signs late or partially — Hormuz normalization shifts to Q3-Q4 2026 at best, Israeli coalition transition proceeds but the election takes months, and conflict intensity falls without structural breakthrough.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (24%):</strong> MOU signed before EOD June 7, mine removal clock starts, Knesset dissolution proceeds on schedule, and Hormuz normalization begins — though ADNOC's 4-month recovery minimum caps any near-term flow restoration.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRIB, Press TV, Tasnim, Mehr) maintained a dual-track narrative through May 19–31: Iran as legitimate defender of sovereign maritime rights in the Strait of Hormuz, and Iran as a constructive party open to dignified negotiations on its own terms. Khamenei's office rejected Trump's nuclear proposal on May 16 as "excessive and outrageous" — this framing received extensive amplification, positioning Iran's posture as principled firmness rather than obstruction. When Trump publicly characterized the deal as "largely negotiated" on May 23, the Iranian MFA described talks as continuing without confirming finality — a posture that serves both internal audiences (avoiding the appearance of capitulation to US pressure) and external negotiating leverage in the ongoing edit-response cycle. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The 60-day MOU framework has not been presented to Iranian domestic audiences as an Iranian concession. State media frames any prospective agreement as a mutual de-escalation preserving Iranian rights in the strait "under Omani coordination" — a formulation that may create a gap between what Iran has agreed to in the draft text and what Khamenei's office will confirm publicly. The MOU's provision that Iran remove mines within 30 days and accept "unrestricted" Hormuz passage had not been publicly acknowledged by IRIB as of May 31, consistent with a pattern of internal negotiation over how to frame any eventual announcement as Iranian agency rather than Iranian retreat. FM Araghchi's active engagement — including Qatar-channel talks around May 24 — has been covered domestically as "Iran maintaining diplomatic initiative," not as Iranian responsiveness to US demands. This internal framing constraint is a structural factor in the principal-confirmation timeline: Pezeshkian's office confirming any text publicly carries domestic political cost that requires careful sequencing. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Coverage of the Israeli coalition crisis and the Haredi conscription dispute received virtually no attention in Iranian state media during this period. Iranian reporting on Lebanon focused consistently on Hezbollah's continued operations as "resistance," framing IDF strikes as Israeli violations without addressing Hezbollah's own fire. The broader arc of Lebanese state institutional collapse under the weight of the conflict — documented by Middle East Monitor (May 28) — was absent from Iranian state narratives, which maintained a framing of Israeli military failure rather than Lebanese institutional deterioration. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

